          "Statutory Reporting & Footnote Disclosures: Under Ind AS 36.126-130 and Schedule III of the Companies Act 2013, an entity must disclose in the notes to financial statements: - The amount of impairment losses recognized in profit or loss during the period and the line item of the Statement of Profit and Loss in which those impairment losses are included (typically Exceptional Items or Operating Expenses). - For each material impairment loss recognized for an individual asset or a CGU: the events and circumstances that led to the recognition; the amount of the impairment loss; for an individual asset, the nature of the asset; for a CGU, a description of the unit and how the unit was aggregated. - Whether the recoverable amount of the asset (CGU) is its fair value less costs of disposal or its value in use. If recoverable amount is fair value less costs of disposal, the level of the fair value hierarchy (Level 1, 2, or 3) within which the fair value measurement is categorized. If recoverable amount is value in use, the key assumptions on which management has based its cash flow projections, the growth rates used to extrapolate cash flow projections, and the pre-tax discount rate applied. - Under Clause 3(i)(a) of CARO 2020, statutory auditors must explicitly examine whether proper records showing full particulars, including quantitative details and situation of PPE and intangible assets, have been maintained, and verify that impairment tests comply with Ind AS 36. Under Standard on Auditing (SA) 540 (Auditing Accounting Estimates and Related Disclosures), the engagement team evaluates the reasonableness of management's revenue growth rates, terminal value multipliers, and WACC discount rate inputs. Under Ind AS 36.134, if a reasonably possible change in a key assumption would cause the unit's carrying amount to exceed its recoverable amount, management must disclose the amount by which the unit's recoverable amount exceeds its carrying amount, the value assigned to the key assumption, and the amount by which the value assigned to the key assumption must change in order for the recoverable amount to be equal to the carrying amount. Furthermore, under SEBI LODR Regulation 33, listed corporations must submit audit-reviewed quarterly financial results where exceptional impairment charges exceeding materiality thresholds are accompanied by an explanatory note from the Board Audit Committee. Our automated platform guarantees that every calculation, from DCF discounting to pro-rata loss distribution, complies with these regulatory standards, establishing an auditable trail of balance sheet integrity across all manufacturing segments and operating subsidiaries.",
